GCC 3-Stage Bootstrap - Core Concept Demo
==============================================

Compiling the demo...
Running the demo...

=== 3-Stage Bootstrap Demonstration ===

Source code: "hello	world  this	is	  gcc"

Stage 0 (system compiler "system-cc"):
  Output: "hello	world  this	is	  gcc"
  Optimizations: 0

Stage 1 (GCC built by system compiler "gcc-stage1"):
  Output: "hello	world  this	is	  gcc"
  Optimizations: 4

Stage 2 (GCC built by Stage 1 "gcc-stage2"):
  Output: "hello	world  this	is	  gcc"
  Optimizations: 4

Stage 3 (GCC built by Stage 2 "gcc-stage3"):
  Output: "hello	world  this	is	  gcc"
  Optimizations: 4

=== Bootstrap Verification ===
Stage 2 output: "hello	world  this	is	  gcc"
Stage 3 output: "hello	world  this	is	  gcc"

✓ PASS: Stage 2 == Stage 3
  The compiler has reached a fixed point.
  Stage 3 is the "true" compiler.

=== Why This Works ===

If the compiler is correct:
  Stage 2 = compile(source, correct_stage1) = correct_output
  Stage 3 = compile(source, correct_stage2) = correct_output
  → Stage 2 == Stage 3 ✓

If the compiler has a bug:
  Stage 2 = compile(source, buggy_stage1) = buggy_output
  Stage 3 = compile(source, buggy_stage2) = ??
  → Most bugs produce DIFFERENT output each generation
  → Stage 2 != Stage 3 → Bug detected! ✗

Only if the bug is "stable" (produces itself) would
Stage 2 == Stage 3 with a buggy compiler. This is rare.
